        <h2>Tea Factory Tours — The Hill Country's Other Attraction</h2>

        <p>Nuwara Eliya produces what many consider Ceylon's finest teas — high-altitude growing conditions create a distinctive bright, brisk, golden-liquor character that's markedly different from the fuller-bodied teas grown at lower elevations. Two factories are especially good for visitors:</p>

        <ul>
          <li><strong>Pedro Tea Estate</strong> — a working estate with regular guided tours showing the full process: plucking, withering, rolling, oxidation, firing, grading. The factory itself is beautifully maintained, and the surrounding fields offer some of the best walk-through-the-tea-estate scenery in the region.</li>
          <li><strong>Damro Labookellie</strong> — on the main road from Kandy to Nuwara Eliya, making it an easy stop on the way in. Well-organised tours, excellent tasting room, and a viewpoint over the fields that most visitors photograph as their Sri Lanka screensaver.</li>
        </ul>

        <p>The best time to visit either factory for active plucking is the morning — tea plucking typically happens in the early hours. Tamil estate workers in brightly coloured saris and headbands moving through an unbroken sea of vivid green is one of the iconic images of Sri Lanka and worth timing your arrival around.</p>

        <h2>Victoria Park & Gregory Lake</h2>

        <p><strong>Victoria Park</strong>, in the centre of town, is a surprisingly lovely public garden with well-maintained flower beds, a stream and excellent birdwatching — the park is known for the Indian blue robin and the Kashmir flycatcher, a rare winter migrant that birders come specifically to see. An hour here in the morning, before the day warms up and the mist burns off the surrounding hills, is a genuinely pleasant way to start a Nuwara Eliya day.</p>

        <p><strong>Gregory Lake</strong>, at the eastern edge of town, has a lakeside promenade for evening strolls, pedal boats and horse rides available on weekends, and a backdrop of tea-covered hills that's particularly photogenic in the late afternoon golden light. The combination of the lake, the hills and the colonial buildings visible on the ridge makes for one of the most characteristic Nuwara Eliya scenes.</p>

        <h2>How to Do Nuwara Eliya in a Day (and Why You Should Stay Longer)</h2>

        <p>Most visitors pass through Nuwara Eliya on the highland circuit — Kandy, Nuwara Eliya, Ella — and give it half a day. That's enough to see the post office, drive past the Grand Hotel and tick the box. But it undersells the place considerably.</p>

        <p>Two nights is the right amount of time. On the first day: arrive on the train from Kandy via Nanu Oya (one of the most scenic rail journeys in Asia), check in, explore the colonial town centre in the late afternoon. Evening: dinner at The Hill Club if you've booked ahead. On the second day: tea factory tour in the morning (Pedro or Labookellie), Hakgala Botanical Gardens and Seetha Amman Temple in the afternoon, Gregory Lake at dusk. Day three: day trip to <a href="/places/horton-plains">Horton Plains and World's End</a> — the plateau is 30 minutes from town and is one of Sri Lanka's great walks.</p>

        <h2>Getting There</h2>
        <p>The nearest train station is <strong>Nanu Oya</strong> (8km from town) — take a tuk-tuk or bus into Nuwara Eliya from there. The train from Kandy to Nanu Oya takes 2.5–3 hours on the scenic highland railway and passes through spectacular tea country. From Ella, the train back to Nanu Oya takes about 2 hours. Buses run directly from Colombo (5 hours) and from Kandy. If you're travelling by tuk-tuk from Ella, the road over the hills is beautiful but slow — allow 2+ hours.</p>

        <h2>What to Pack</h2>
        <p>Pack warmer than you think you need. Night temperatures in Nuwara Eliya drop to 8–10°C even in the dry season, and cold mist rolls in without warning. A fleece, a light waterproof jacket and long trousers are essential. If you're continuing to Horton Plains, mornings on the plateau can be 5–7°C — genuinely cold by any standard. The contrast with the coast is dramatic and most visitors are caught underprepared.</p>

